{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1782269700,"nonce":"EzyrGttrtnvMn24y+nbZ4uVuSg4=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABGgEpLA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/318","origin":"github"},"title":"Live battery SOC estimator (hybrid PWM-coulomb + voltage anchoring)","message":"## Motivation\n\nThe 2026-06-23 power loss ([#313](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/313)) showed the operator has **no live state-of-charge** — only raw voltage, which is unreliable mid-range on the LiFePO4 plateau. By the time the 23 V annunciator fired, the boat was already past the point of no return ([#315](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/315)). A live SOC % would give early, actionable awareness and is the input the [#315](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/315) distance-aware turn-back reserve rule needs (remaining Ah → remaining range vs distance home).\n\nThe boat has **no current sensor** (`BATT_MONITOR 3`, voltage-only), so true coulomb counting isn't available — but the **quadratic PWM-coulomb model is now validated** by the 06-23 full discharge (predicted 272.6 Ah, pack emptied at 273 Ah nameplate; see [#167](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/167)). That makes a software SOC estimator feasible with no hardware.\n\n## Proposed: hybrid PWM-coulomb + voltage-anchored SOC estimator\n\nA small live node:\n\n1. **Reset to full** from resting voltage at start (loaded/resting V ≥ ~28.7–29 V ⇒ SOC = 100 % ≈ 273 Ah).\n2. **Integrate modeled current** from throttle: `I_model = 8 A idle + 59 A·(|pwm−1500|/500)²` over `rc/out` (the validated quad model). `Ah_used += I_model·dt`; `SOC% = 100·(1 − Ah_used / C_usable)`.\n3. **Re-anchor on voltage in the knee** — voltage is trustworthy near full and near empty. When the 60-s loaded voltage crosses the ladder thresholds (23 V / 22 V / 21.5 V), snap the SOC estimate toward the ladder's known charge-left (OCV correction, like a real fuel gauge).\n4. **Publish** SOC %, remaining Ah, and a remaining-range estimate as topics + an annunciator gauge.\n\n## Known limitations (must handle)\n\n- **Open-loop drift** — no current feedback; errors in the 8 A idle / 59 A anchors / quad exponent / variable hotel load (cameras, comms) accumulate. The voltage re-anchoring (step 3) bounds it. One validation point so far (06-23); needs a few full cycles to confirm.\n- **Usable capacity is not fixed** — 273 Ah is nameplate; temperature/age shift it. Tune `C_usable` from a few full discharges; re-anchor on voltage.\n- **Hotel-load uncertainty** — relates to [#196](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/196) (charger idle ~6.2 A); a measured idle improves the floor.\n\n## Robust alternative (hardware, longer-term)\n\nWire a **current shunt/hall sensor** on the pack output → `BATT_MONITOR 4` + `BATT_CAPACITY 273000` gives ArduPilot **native** coulomb counting and a real `BatteryState.percentage` (currently a hardcoded placeholder; `bizzyboat_hardware.md` notes `BATT_CURR_PIN` was set as if a sensor existed but none is wired). The software estimator here is the **no-hardware interim**; the sensor is the real fix. (Current sensing is also flagged under [#88](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/88).)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Live SOC %, remaining Ah, remaining range published and shown on the annunciator.\n- Validated against ≥2–3 full or partial discharges (coulomb endpoint vs voltage knee).\n- Feeds the [#315](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/315) reserve rule.\n\nRelates to #315, #167, #88, #196, #313.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}